>> title: Asazuke
>> category: Toppings & Pickles
>> tags: japanese, cucumber, cabbage, pickle, make ahead, no-cook, vegetarian
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: 浅漬け, asazuke, asazuke, あさづけ, きゅうりの浅漬け, kyuri no asazuke, quick pickle, overnight pickle
>> pairs-with: gohan, gyudon
>> servings: 4
>> time: 4 hr 10 min
>> slack: forgiving — past three days it goes soft and starts to smell of the fridge, and nothing brings an asazuke back from that

>> step: Salt drawing water out over hours, not lactic acid over weeks. It lives in the fridge and is eaten in days.
The shallow pickle, and the difference from the fermented kind is the whole file.

>> step: salt and rub 2 min
Rub @cucumbers{2}(quartered lengthways and cut into 1-inch pieces) and @napa cabbage{7%oz}(200 g; in 1-inch squares) with @fine sea salt{2%tsp}(about 2 percent of the weight of the vegetables) in a #bowl{} ~rub{2%min}, until they darken and a pool starts under them.

>> step: weight it, 4 hr in the fridge
Add @kombu{1%piece}(2 in, snipped into threads with scissors), @dried red chile{1}(seeded, in rings) and @ginger{1%piece}(1 in, in matchsticks) to @&(~1)salted vegetables{}, press a plate and a weight on top, and ~press{4%hr} in the fridge.

>> step: wring out before it goes to the table
Wring @&(~1)asazuke{} out by the handful as it goes into the bowl.
